    isbefore: []  # f37b
    duplicate_of: []  # f37b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f37b
    pr_ref:  # f37b
    description: "FOUND BY CHECKING THE PROBE RATHER THAN TRUSTING IT. Four of the 17 rust modules\nWI-lutuh's audit refused were refused on a RESOLUTION ARTIFACT, not on evidence\nabout the module. Refusing is the safe direction so nothing is broken, but it is\nnot an adjudication and it silently costs coverage the audit exists to produce.\n\n  std::net::SocketAddr   Resolved to `std/src/os/unix/net/addr.rs` -- the UNIX\n                         DOMAIN SOCKET address type, a DIFFERENT type that\n                         happens to share the name. The real one is\n                         `core/src/net/socket_addr.rs`. The probe greps for\n                         `pub struct <Name>` and takes the first hit in sort\n                         order; a `/sys/` exclusion was added after SystemTime\n                         resolved to a HermitOS internal, and `/os/unix/` is the\n                         same bug with a different prefix.\n  std::io::ErrorKind     Resolved correctly to `std/src/io/error.rs`, but the\n                         only signal is `pub type RawOsError = sys::io::RawOsError;`\n                         -- a SIBLING declaration in the same file. ErrorKind\n                         itself is a plain enum with no I/O surface.\n  std::ptr, std::slice   Refused on a bare `metadata` regex match that is\n                         `ptr::metadata` -- POINTER metadata (DynMetadata), not\n                         filesystem metadata. Cited lines:\n                         `from_raw_parts::<U>(self as *const (), metadata(meta))`\n                         and `ptr::metadata(self)`.\n\nWHAT TO DO: re-adjudicate these four with the resolution corrected. ptr and slice\nare already judged grantable on the cited false-positive evidence and were\ncounted in the audit's 16; SocketAddr and ErrorKind are genuinely unadjudicated.\n\nAND FIX THE PROBE, because the artifact will recur: type resolution must exclude\nplatform- and target-specific paths generally (`/sys/`, `/os/<platform>/`), not\none prefix at a time, and should prefer a definition whose module path MATCHES\nthe requested path rather than taking the first grep hit. Instrument:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_rust_08262026/probe.py.\n\nTHE GENERAL LESSON, worth keeping because it cost three rounds in one sitting:\nevery probe bug found in this audit produced a FALSE ALL-CLEAR or a false\nrefusal, and every one was caught by disbelieving an implausible intermediate\n(a 1-file count for std::collections, a CANDIDATE verdict for bare `std`, a\nCANDIDATE for BufReader), never by the probe reporting an error."  # f37b
    fields: {}  # f37b
- op: discuss  # bd73
  at: '2026-08-27T08:34:21Z'  # bd73
  by: agent  # bd73
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bd73
  clock: 2  # bd73
  nonce: bd73  # bd73
  message: "ADJUDICATED, AND THE PROBE FIXED. Instrument, controls and artifact:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_rust_08262026/{probe.py, typescope.py,\ncheck_probe.py, probe4_dukom.json}; the pre-fix probe is kept beside them as\nprobe_pre_dukom.py so the A/B is reproducible.\n\nTHE FOUR VERDICTS\n\n  std::net::SocketAddr   GRANTABLE. It is a value type: 7 pub fns (new, ip,\n      set_ip, port, set_port, is_ipv4, is_ipv6) plus Display/Debug/From. On the\n      std side it has EXACTLY ONE impl, and it is the identity:\n          impl ToSocketAddrs for SocketAddr {\n              fn to_socket_addrs(&self) -> io::Result<..> {\n                  Ok(Some(*self).into_iter()) } }\n      The DNS that reaches sys::net::lookup_host lives in `impl ToSocketAddrs\n      for str` and `for (&str, u16)` -- a DIFFERENT RECEIVER TYPE, so it is not\n      SocketAddr's boundary. Filed separately as the uncatalogued str/DNS gap.\n\n  std::io::ErrorKind     GRANTABLE. A plain enum: ZERO pub fns, one\n      `pub(crate) fn as_str`. The `sys::` it was refused on is\n      `pub type RawOsError = sys::io::RawOsError;` -- a sibling declaration in\n      the same file, belonging to `Error`, not to ErrorKind.\n\n  std::ptr, std::slice   GRANTABLE, CONFIRMED. Both were refused on a bare\n      `metadata` that matched `ptr::metadata` -- the non-address half of a fat\n      pointer, nothing to do with a filesystem. A deliberately OVER-BROAD net\n      (sys|libc|syscall|fd|Fd|Handle|open|close|socket|connect|lookup|File|io::)\n      finds ZERO hits in either module's 7 and 23 files. NOTE, because a\n      name-keyed matcher could trip on it: std::ptr publicly exports `read`,\n      `write`, `copy`, `read_volatile`, `write_unaligned`. Granting ptr\n      `complete` is not merely safe here, it is PROTECTIVE -- it makes those\n      calls an examined negative instead of leaving the spelling to be guessed.\n\nTHE PROBE HAD FIVE BUGS, NOT ONE, and the fourth and fifth were found by\ndisbelieving the run rather than by any error:\n\n 1. A TYPE IS NOT A FILE, and the unit was wrong in BOTH directions. TOO BIG\n    for ErrorKind (refused for a sibling's declaration); TOO SMALL for\n    SocketAddr, which is DEFINED in core as a pure value type while its\n    ToSocketAddrs impls live in std -- read the definition file alone and a DNS\n    boundary is invisible, which is the false-all-clear direction. A type is\n    now read as its OWN ITEMS (declaration, inherent impls, trait impls for it)\n    across every agreeing file.\n 2. FIRST GREP HIT IN SORT ORDER IS NOT A RESOLUTION. Platform/target trees\n    (/src/sys/, /src/os/) are now excluded generally rather than one prefix per\n    bug, and survivors are RANKED by module-path agreement.\n 3. A MODULE CAN BE A FILE AND A DIRECTORY AT ONCE. `std/src/fs.rs` is the\n    2000-line module; `std/src/fs/` beside it holds only `tests.rs`, and the\n    is_dir() test fired first -- so std::fs and std::process were each\n    adjudicated against THEIR OWN TEST FILE and nothing else. Both still\n    refused (the tests name File and Command because they exercise them), so it\n    cost nothing here; a module with thin tests would have read as having no\n    surface while its real source sat unopened.\n 4. IMPORTS. Scoping to a type's items drops `use crate::sys::{.., time};`, and\n    SystemTime reaches the clock through that alias -- it read CANDIDATE.\n 5. DEREF. PathBuf's own items are pure string work; it DEREFS to Path, so\n    `pb.read_dir()` is a PathBuf call site. It read CANDIDATE too.\n\nTHREE OF THE FIVE FIXES NARROW what is scanned or counted, which is the\nfalse-all-clear direction, so each was applied ALONE and measured across all 33\nmodules against a control set of 15 must-refuse and 6 must-candidate. THAT\nCONTROL SET IS WHY 4 AND 5 EXIST: item-scoping alone turned a clock read and a\ntype you can call read_dir() on into grantable candidates. Both would have been\ngranted. Final harness: 3 resolution checks + 24 controls, all pass.\n\nNET EFFECT, v0 -> final, over the same 33 modules: CANDIDATE 14 -> 18, and the\nfour movers are EXACTLY the four this item filed. Nine further modules changed\nWHICH FILES they scan without changing their verdict, which is the reassuring\nshape. The 18 candidates are input to WI-lutuh's grants; the probe still never\ngrants -- each one needs the source in hand, as these four just got.\n\nTRANSFERABLE, AND IT SHARPENS WI-gihos'S WRAPPER RULE. There are TWO wrapper\nrelationships and they point OPPOSITE ways. A type that WRAPS a generic\n(BufReader<R>) has an unknowable boundary and gets no rows. A type that DEREFS\nto a concrete target (PathBuf -> Path, String -> str) INHERITS the target's\nmethod surface at the call site and must carry, or be refused for, whatever the\ntarget carries. Deref-target inclusion stops at a type PARAMETER: Arc<T> and\nCow<'_, B> name a generic, and single-letter targets never recurse."  # bd73
